Title: "Natural Numbers"
Id: "acc9e650-ac98-4a0b-821a-5bc440a9388e"


Text: "The natural numbers are the numbers of counting: zero, one, two, and so
       on without end. This chapter builds them from the ground already laid."
Id: "3079172c-5778-44bd-9391-60ce0f0b12e7"


SectionTitle: "The Peano Axioms"
Id: "1bf121c7-8539-4a95-8870-6ef558c2a240"


[\naturals]
Defines: Nb ::= (N, 0, S(n_))
abstractly:
specifies:
. N is \set
. 0 "in" N
. S is \function:on{N}:to{N}
expresses:
. forAll: n "in" N
  then: S(n) != 0
. forAll: m, n "in" N
  then: (.S(m) = S(n).) \.implies./ (.m = n.)
. forAll: A \:subset:/ N
  then:
  . if:
    . 0 "in" A
    . forAll: n "in" A
      then: S(n) "in" A
    then: A = N
Enables:
. capability: n_ "in" Nb :-> n_ is \natural
. view:
  as: S := N is \set
Documented:
. called: "the naturals"
. written: "\mathbb{N}"
. description: "The natural numbers $(\mathbb{N}, 0, S)$ axiomatized via the
                Peano postulates: $0$ is not a successor, the successor function
                $S$ is injective, and induction holds."
Id: "502e2a50-cced-461a-bdcf-03e2221a598f"


[\natural]
Declares: n "in" \naturals..N
Enables:
. capability: n_ + m_ :=> n_ \.natural.+./ m_
Documented:
. called: "natural"
. description: "A natural number is a member of the underlying set $\mathbb{N}$."
Id: "6c240df6-c06d-4fc4-ab16-31fa5aebb4d7"


SectionTitle: "Constants and Arithmetic"
Id: "d11de551-8fdb-461b-bb19-787caa038d69"


Text: "The initial natural number is zero, and its successor is one."
Id: "428700ea-91a3-4f0a-9418-1e0bd3a8c0d7"


[\natural.zero]
Defines: z := \naturals..0 is \natural
Documented:
. called: "natural zero"
. written: "0"
. description: "The distinguished element $0 \in \mathbb{N}$."
Id: "3c4f37ab-1bff-4a4a-90ea-8f808863dbfc"


[\natural.one]
Defines: o := \naturals..S(\natural.zero) is \natural
Documented:
. called: "natural one"
. written: "1"
. description: "The natural number $1$, defined as the successor $S(0)$."
Id: "8ced2d1f-741d-4996-84f8-b2c2afd293e8"


Text: "Addition of natural numbers is defined recursively: adding zero leaves a
       number unchanged, and adding a successor steps forward by successor."
Id: "f4dd2fa3-9c90-4e21-ac5e-ef4f4052e599"


[n_ \.natural.+./ m_]
Defines: n_ + m_ is \binary.operation:on{\naturals}
expresses:
. piecewise:
  if: n_ = 0
  then: n_ + m_ := m_
  else:
  . let: k is \natural
    where: n_ = \naturals..S(k)
    then: n_ + m_ := \naturals..S(n_ \.natural.+./ k)
Documented:
. called: "$n_?$ plus $m_?$"
. written: "n_? + m_?"
. description: "Addition of natural numbers defined by recursion on the first
                argument: $0 + m = m$ and $S(k) + m = S(k + m)$."
Id: "a8dda59b-29db-4ac2-8d70-e9010ccae3e7"


Text: "Multiplication is defined by repeated addition via primitive recursion."
Id: "2334b955-ab67-4bb6-baf5-b7f8e72cae55"


[n_ \.natural.*./ m_]
Defines: n_ * m_ is \binary.operation:on{\naturals}
expresses:
. piecewise:
  if: n_ = 0
  then: n_ * m_ := 0
  else:
  . let: k is \natural
    where: n_ = \naturals..S(k)
    then: n_ * m_ := (k \.natural.*./ m_) \.natural.+./ m_
Documented:
. called: "$n_?$ times $m_?$"
. written: "n_? \cdot m_?"
. description: "Multiplication of natural numbers defined by recursion:
                $0 \cdot m = 0$ and $S(k) \cdot m = (k \cdot m) + m$."
Id: "46c21a80-12cd-45f6-8e74-07731d18d220"


SectionTitle: "Order on the Natural Numbers"
Id: "c2be9441-a886-483d-ac05-cbf63c49eb49"


Text: "A natural number $n$ is less than or equal to $m$ if $m$ can be reached
       from $n$ by adding some natural number $k$."
Id: "95c0a40d-4f64-4ab4-a7c4-fbc86a103719"


[n \:natural.leq:/ m]
Declares: n is \natural
when: m is \natural
satisfies:
. exists: k is \natural
  suchThat: n \.natural.+./ k = m
Documented:
. called: "$n?$ is less than or equal to $m?$"
. written: "n? \leq m?"
. description: "The canonical order on natural numbers: $n \leq m$ if and only
                if there exists $k \in \mathbb{N}$ such that $n + k = m$."
Id: "8c997826-bcef-4032-816b-c6dd2aa635e7"
